{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## First `make verify` FAILED: exit 2, 33 failed / 19,817 passed. Root-caused, not retried.\n\nTwo process failures of my own, first, because they made my interim reports worthless:\n\n1. **The background-task notification said \"exit code 0\". That was the WRAPPER's exit, not\n   make's.** My own `echo $?` captured the truth — `VERIFY_EXIT=2`. This is exactly the trap\n   the brief warned about, arriving from a direction I had not anticipated.\n2. **I polled progress with `grep -cE \"^(FAILED|ERROR)\"` and reported \"zero failures\" for\n   over an hour.** pytest writes ANSI colour codes before `FAILED`, so that pattern could\n   never match. The grep was not measuring the property I claimed it was — the same defect I\n   am supposed to be perturbing tests to catch. Every \"zero failures\" I reported during the\n   run was unfounded.\n\n## The 33, classified\n\n| count | cause | mine? |\n|---|---|---|\n| 29 | `GateCongestedError: all 4 concurrent gate slot(s) ... in use` | **no** |\n| 1 | `assert 11 == 2` (exit 11 IS the congestion retry code) | no — same cause |\n| 2 | error-code taxonomy contract rejecting `GateMemoryLowError` | **yes** |\n| 1 | `subprocess.TimeoutExpired: ['claude', 'mcp', 'list']` | no — environmental |\n\n## The 29: root cause established by measurement\n\nIt is NOT xdist contention — **it reproduces single-threaded** with `-p no:randomly -x`.\n\nThe slot directory is host-wide: `/var/folders/.../T/rebar-gate-snapshots/locks/gate-slot-N`.\n`lsof` on those files during the failure named the holders, and none were mine:\n\n    python3.1  7168  .../comment-gates-current/.venv/bin/rebar review-plan sphalerite-bereft-tortoise\n    python3.1 14552  .../fix-caused-by-lagging-checkout/.venv/bin/pytest -n 4 ...\n\nAnother agent's plan-review and another agent's test run, in other worktrees, holding the\nslots. This is the saturation the operator warned about, reaching the suite.\n\n**PROVED, not asserted:** re-running all eight affected files with `REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR` pointed\nat a private directory gives **87 passed, 0 failed** — including all 29. The full re-verify is\nrunning under that same isolation, which controls the environmental variable without changing\nanything under test (the slot files' location is all that moves).\n\nFiled as bug `unfleshly-peerless-grayfox` (863f-52ef-c3a8-4794), linked `discovered_from` this\nstory. `test_gate_admission_09da.py` does not have the problem — its fixture already sets\n`REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR` — which is what makes the fix obvious for the other eight.\n\n## The 2 that ARE mine, and the design change they forced\n\nAdding `GateMemoryLowError` broke `test_error_code_taxonomy_contract.py`: a new `LLMError`\nsubclass must be registered in `src/rebar/_errors.py`, and separately in the CLI's\n`_gate_admission_errors()` tuple or a memory refusal would exit 1 instead of 11.\n\n**Neither file is in this ticket's `file_impact`, and widening it would stale the\nattestation.** But convenience is not a reason, so I checked whether the separate class was\nright to begin with — and it was not. I had already argued, for the journald MARKER, that\n\"both refusals are the same operational event\" and reused the existing token. A separate\nexception class contradicted my own reasoning, and the contract failure is what that\ninconsistency looks like from outside.\n\nSo `GateMemoryLowError` is **removed**. Memory refusals now raise `GateCongestedError` with\n`reason=\"memory_low\"` plus `available_mib` / `floor_mib` fields. Consequences:\n\n- The `gate_congested` error code, the retryable outcome, exit 11, the `except LLMError` arms\n  and the `GATE_CONGESTED` marker ALL apply with no seam taught anything new. The\n  `assert 11 == 2` failure is precisely what a forgotten seam looks like.\n- No file outside the declared impact is touched; the attestation stays valid.\n- The cause is still fully distinguishable, as structured data rather than as a type.\n\nPerturbation of the new discriminator (run against the WHOLE file after an earlier `-k`\nfilter silently selected the wrong two tests and passed under perturbation — caught and\nredone): pinning `reason` to `slots_full`, pinning it to `memory_low`, and dropping\n`available_mib` each fail exactly one test. Restored: 41 passed.\n\n`make lint` and `make typecheck` are green again."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788669171008454001,"uuid":"34479155-3eda-4cff-a843-f913952dcf10"}